{"ex-E203-1":{"number":"1","title":"Surface-area-to-volume ratio","question":"<p>(a) Find the surface-area-to-volume ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of a cube of side length 1 cm.</p><p>(b) Find the surface-area-to-volume ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of a cube of side length 1 nm.</p><p>(c) Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> : <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>total surface area</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>volume</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 600 m<sup>–1</sup></p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>total surface area</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>volume</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 6 × 10<sup>9</sup> m<sup>–1</sup></p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> : <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 6 × 10<sup>9</sup> : 600 = 10<sup>7</sup> : 1</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>A cube has 6 surfaces.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E2  |  Chapter E203  |  Source PDF p. 8","source":{"name":"E203 Examples.docx","sha256":"dc98a13319d127e5fd8a49b01368c6661cf62acb2510c0b8d1da424796377e13","block":2},"id":"ex-E203-1","chapter":"E203"},"ex-E203-2":{"number":"2","title":"Dividing a cube","question":"<p>A cube of side length 1 cm is divided into cubes of side length (a) 0.5 cm,</p><p>(b) 1 nm. What is the ratio of the total surface area after dividing to that before dividing in each case?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Number of cubes on each side = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2</p><p>∴ Total number of cubes after dividing = (2)<sup>3</sup> = 8</p><p>Ratio of total surface area = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2</p><p>(b) Number of cubes on each side = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 10<sup>7</sup></p><p>∴ Total number of cubes after dividing = (10<sup>7</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = 10<sup>21</sup></p><p>Ratio of total surface area = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 10<sup>7</sup></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/b5513f9cd304e2df2aa9c1d6.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:174.8px\"/></p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/c3d39ff6ebacf9a4bee4fd6e.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:180.9px\"/></p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E2  |  Chapter E203  |  Source PDF p. 8","source":{"name":"E203 Examples.docx","sha256":"dc98a13319d127e5fd8a49b01368c6661cf62acb2510c0b8d1da424796377e13","block":14},"id":"ex-E203-2","chapter":"E203"},"ex-E203-3":{"number":"3","title":"Resolving power of the eye","question":"<p>Stuart’s eye is 25 cm from an object. The wavelength of the light reflected from the object is 500 nm and the diameter of Stuart’s pupil is 5 mm.</p><p>(a) Estimate the angular resolution θ<sub>R</sub> of Stuart’s eye by considering the Rayleigh criterion.</p><p>(b) Estimate minimum distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> between two points on the object which can be resolved by Stuart’s eye.</p><p>(c) If Stuart is further away from the object, how do θ<sub>R</sub> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> change?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) θ<sub>R</sub> ≈ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.22 × 10<sup>−4</sup> rad</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ≈ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>θ <sub>R</sub> = (0.25)(1.22 × 10<sup>−4</sup>) = 3.05 × 10<sup>−5</sup> m</p><p>(c) θ<sub>R</sub> remains unchanged as it is independent of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> increases as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> increases according to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ≈ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>θ <sub>R</sub>.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E2  |  Chapter E203  |  Source PDF p. 19","source":{"name":"E203 Examples.docx","sha256":"dc98a13319d127e5fd8a49b01368c6661cf62acb2510c0b8d1da424796377e13","block":30},"id":"ex-E203-3","chapter":"E203"},"ex-E203-4":{"number":"4","title":"Resolving power of a camera","question":"<p>Two lamps 1 m apart emits light of wavelength 590 nm. A camera is 9 km away. Its aperture has a diameter of 6.2 mm.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/da27efb8b5c9da4950002ef6.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:401.1px\"/></p><p>(a) What is the angular resolution θ<sub>R</sub> of the camera according to the Rayleigh criterion?</p><p>(b) The lamps cannot be resolved by the camera. Explain.</p><p>(c) Suggest one method that allows the lamps to be resolved by the camera.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) θ<sub>R</sub> ≈ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.16 × 10<sup>−4</sup> rad</p><p>(b) Minimum distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> that can be resolved by the camera</p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>θ <sub>R</sub> = (9000)(1.16 × 10<sup>−4</sup>) = 1.04 m</p><p>Since the distance between the lamps is shorter than 1.04 m, they cannot be resolved by the camera.</p><p>(c) Move the camera closer to the lamps. / Increase the distance between the lamps.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E2  |  Chapter E203  |  Source PDF p. 19","source":{"name":"E203 Examples.docx","sha256":"dc98a13319d127e5fd8a49b01368c6661cf62acb2510c0b8d1da424796377e13","block":40},"id":"ex-E203-4","chapter":"E203"},"ex-E203-5":{"number":"5","title":"Resolving power of TEM","question":"<p>In a TEM, an accelerating voltage of 40 kV is applied across the cathode and the anode in the electron gun. The initial KE of electrons emitted from the cathode is negligible. Take the charge and mass of an electron as −1.60 × 10<sup>−19</sup> C and 9.11 × 10<sup>−31</sup> kg respectively, and Planck constant as</p><p>6.63 × 10<sup>−34</sup> J s.</p><p>(a) When the electron leaves the electron gun, find its speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and de Broglie wavelength λ.</p><p>(b) Estimate the angular resolution θ<sub>R</sub> of the TEM by considering the Rayleigh criterion. Take the diameter of the aperture as 10 cm.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Gain in KE = loss in electric